    <h1>How we score and select tools</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-ink-300 leading-relaxed">
      OpenSourceStack is editorial — meaning every project is reviewed by a human editor before it ships. This page explains exactly what that means: how we discover projects, what we test, how the security and quality scores are calculated, and how we handle removals and disputes.
    </p>

    <h2>1. How a project gets listed</h2>
    <p>
      Projects enter our consideration through four channels: our team finding them in release notes, GitHub trending, Hacker News, or maintainer newsletters; community submissions via our contact form; maintainer outreach (a project owner emails us saying "please consider us"); and word-of-mouth from other editors.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every candidate goes through the same four-step process:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Discovered.</strong> We log the project in our internal tracker with the source (where we found it), the maintainer's GitHub, the license, and the project's stated maturity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Self-hosted.</strong> We run the install command in a clean Docker environment with the recommended setup. If the install doesn't work in 30 minutes following the official docs, the project doesn't ship. This single test eliminates about 30% of candidates — many open source projects have broken or outdated install instructions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tested.</strong> We exercise the core use case. For a database, we create a schema and run real queries. For a chat platform, we send real messages between two test users. For analytics, we install the tracking pixel and verify the dashboard reports the events. We test the failure modes — what happens if the disk fills up, what happens if the network drops, what happens at 10x expected load.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reviewed.</strong> We write a hand-typed description of what it does, what it's good for, and where it falls down. The Security and Quality scores are set by the reviewer at this stage, not auto-generated.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>2. Security Score (0–100)</h2>
    <p>
      The Security Score answers one question: <em>is this safe to run with real data?</em> It's a holistic judgment by the reviewer, informed by:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>CVE history.</strong> How many known vulnerabilities has the project had, how quickly were they patched, and how many are currently unpatched. We check CVE databases and the project's security advisory feed.</li>
      <li><strong>Auth model maturity.</strong> Does the project have real authentication (RBAC, MFA, SSO) or just a single shared password? Does it default to no auth (a red flag for production)?</li>
      <li><strong>Default network exposure.</strong> Does the project bind to 0.0.0.0 by default or only 127.0.0.1? Does it require explicit opt-in for public access? Are there clear warnings if you expose it?</li>
      <li><strong>Encryption.</strong> Is data encrypted at rest? In transit? With what algorithms? Is key management built-in or do you have to roll your own?</li>
      <li><strong>Patch cadence.</strong> How quickly are security patches released after disclosure? A project that takes 90+ days to patch CVEs is riskier than one that takes 7 days, even if both eventually patch.</li>
      <li><strong>Dependency hygiene.</strong> How many transitive dependencies does the project have? Are they pinned? Are there known vulnerabilities in the dependency tree?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A score of <strong>90+</strong> means we'd run this in production with sensitive customer data. A score of <strong>75-89</strong> means it's fine for non-sensitive workloads. A score of <strong>below 75</strong> means "use with caution, sandbox it" — we still list it because options matter, but we make the risk clear.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Quality Score (0–100)</h2>
    <p>
      The Quality Score answers a different question: <em>is this actually good to use?</em> It's about the day-to-day experience of working with the project, informed by:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Documentation completeness.</strong> Are there getting-started guides, API references, troubleshooting pages, deployment guides? Are they up to date or three years stale?</li>
      <li><strong>Error handling.</strong> When something goes wrong, do you get a clear error message with a stack trace and a documented fix, or a cryptic 500 with no context?</li>
      <li><strong>Performance under load.</strong> We run each project through a load test (1x, 10x, and 100x expected production traffic) and note where it starts to break down.</li>
      <li><strong>Maintainer activity.</strong> Are pull requests being reviewed? Are issues being triaged? Is there a public roadmap? A project with 2,000 open issues and no maintainer response in 6 months gets a low Quality Score even if the code is technically clean.</li>
      <li><strong>Failure mode design.</strong> When something breaks, does the project fail safely? Does it have retry logic, circuit breakers, graceful degradation? Or does it crash and take the whole system down?</li>
      <li><strong>Upgrade experience.</strong> How painful is upgrading from v1 to v2? Are there migration guides? Does the project respect semver?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A score of <strong>95+</strong> is something you'd happily use for years without cursing it. A score of <strong>85-94</strong> works well but has rough edges. A score of <strong>below 85</strong> means you'll find yourself debugging around it weekly.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Status and uptime</h2>
    <p>
      For cloud-hosted projects, we run a daily uptime check from three geographic regions and report the 30-day average. The "Last checked" timestamp is when our most recent probe ran. If a project's cloud version goes down or starts returning errors, the listing updates within an hour and the score is recalculated.
    </p>
    <p>
      For self-hosted projects, the Status column reflects the project's own status page (when they have one) and the activity of the repository. We don't host these projects, so we can't directly measure their uptime — we measure the project's own infrastructure.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Comparison pages</h2>
    <p>
      Every comparison page (URLs like <code>/vs/supabase-vs-directus/</code>) is auto-generated from the underlying listings. Each one has:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>An "Editor's take" intro paragraph (auto-written from the two listings' descriptions)</li>
      <li>Side-by-side cards with security and quality scores</li>
      <li>A "When to pick which" section explaining the use cases each project is best for</li>
      <li>A score breakdown table comparing the metrics</li>
      <li>3 related projects in the same category</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every comparison pair gets both orderings (supabase-vs-directus AND directus-vs-supabase) so users searching in either direction find the page.
    </p>

    <h2>6. What gets a project removed</h2>
    <p>
      A listing is removed if any of these is true:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The project becomes unmaintained (no commits, no releases, no maintainer response to issues for 12+ months)</li>
      <li>The project relicenses to a non-open-source license (AGPL to proprietary, MIT to source-available with restrictions, etc.)</li>
      <li>The project pivots away from its original scope in a way that no longer fits the category</li>
      <li>We discover fraud — fake commits, fake contributors, fabricated security claims</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Removal is rare. We're slower to remove than to add, because a project in transition (e.g., one maintainer stepping back, looking for a new one) often recovers. We err on the side of keeping listings with a clear "limited maintenance" warning rather than removing them.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Sponsored listings</h2>
    <p>
      We accept sponsorships. A sponsored listing gets:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A "Verified" or "Featured" badge on the card</li>
      <li>Optionally, a pin to the top of its category</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Sponsorship does <strong>not</strong> affect:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The Security or Quality score (those are reviewer-set)</li>
      <li>The position in search results (we don't have one)</li>
      <li>The comparison pages (we'd show an inferior sponsored product over a better non-sponsored one, and we have)</li>
      <li>Whether a listing gets removed (if it becomes unmaintained, sponsor or not, it goes)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Sponsorship tiers are $49/mo for Verified, $199/mo for Featured. We list the price on each sponsored listing so readers know. Payment via PayPal.
    </p>

    <h2>8. What we don't do</h2>
    <p>
      To keep trust high, we explicitly avoid:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Affiliate links.</strong> If you click through to a project and they pay us, we'll never know. We don't have affiliate relationships.</li>
      <li><strong>Auto-approval.</strong> Every project is reviewed by a human before it ships. We don't accept bulk submissions or "list everything on GitHub with 100+ stars."</li>
      <li><strong>Pay-to-rank.</strong> Featured placement is editor-decided. The highest Quality Score in a category is always near the top regardless of sponsorship.</li>
      <li><strong>User accounts / comments / reviews.</strong> We deliberately don't have a logged-in experience. The directory is editorial, not crowd-sourced. User reviews are too easily gamed.</li>
      <li><strong>Tracking.</strong> We use Cloudflare Web Analytics (no cookies, no personal data). No Google Analytics, no Facebook Pixel, no third-party trackers of any kind.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. Disputes and corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If you're a project maintainer and disagree with our score or description, email us. We take corrections seriously:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Factual errors get fixed within 48 hours</li>
      <li>Score disputes require evidence (a CVE you think we missed, a perf test that contradicts ours)</li>
      <li>If we got something materially wrong, we publish a correction note on the listing</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>10. Why this matters</h2>
    <p>
      The point of the methodology page is transparency. You should be able to read this and know exactly how the directory works, what its incentives are, and where its blind spots might be. If something here is unclear, or if you have a methodology question we haven't answered, email us — we'll add it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-ink-500">
      Last updated: September 15, 2026. Maintained by the OpenSourceStack editorial team.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
